_READ_IT = object()


def slot_table(path=None):
    """[(instance, type)] exactly as the slot table declares them, or None.

    THE ONE PARSER FOR THAT FILE. There were two readings of it in this module
    -- idle_windows() here, and nothing at all on the mint path, which read a
    directory listing instead -- and the whole of the defect below was that the
    typed authority was consulted only where being wrong was harmless.

    A row is "<index> <instance> [<type>]". The type defaults to "desktop" when
    the column is absent, which is the CLI's own reading of the same file; a
    table written before the column existed describes named desktops, and
    guessing "ephemeral" for them would put a person's long-lived session into
    the pool a stranger is minted onto.

    None, NOT an empty list, when the file cannot be read. The two mean opposite
    things to a caller deciding whether to hand a slot out -- "this box has no
    ephemeral slots configured" is a reason to refuse politely, and "I could not
    read the file that says" is a reason to say so loudly -- and returning [] for
    both is how an unreadable table would have become a silent, permanent 503
    with nothing in the log naming the file.
    """
    path = SLOTS_FILE if path is None else path
    try:
        with open(path, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            raw = f.readlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    rows = []
    for line in raw:
        bits = line.split()
        if len(bits) < 2 or bits[0].startswith("#"):
            continue
        rows.append((bits[1], bits[2] if len(bits) > 2 else "desktop"))
    return rows


def ephemeral_slots(table=_READ_IT):
    """The names a visitor may be minted onto. None if the table is unreadable.

    THE POOL, and the reason this exists rather than a directory listing is the
    defect it replaces. The mint path used to compute free capacity from
    socket_filenames() below -- os.listdir() on the proxy run directory, filtered
    on a ".sock" suffix -- and EVERY proxy instance on the box binds into that
    one directory, named and ephemeral alike. Measured 2026-09-22 and recorded in
    that function's own docstring: "a named session's leftover socket is
    enumerated into the ephemeral pool". Three separate seats sighted it; nothing
    was built on it until now, and a provisioning slot that must be reachable by
    an administrator and never offered to a visitor is exactly the thing that
    directory listing cannot express.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT FILTERED HERE: whether anything is listening. The
    brief that asked for this called the pool an intersection of the typed rows
    with listening_paths(), and that is one condition too many -- it would undo
    pick_slot()'s reap-window reasoning, which is correct and predates this. A
    slot the sweep has just reaped has no desktop behind it and is momentarily
    unreachable, and it is precisely the capacity we want to hand out: its
    socket unit is still listening and the next connection starts a fresh
    desktop. Refusing a visitor while such a slot stood free is the exhaustion
    defect wearing a reachability check. So reachability stays where it already
    was -- a PREFERENCE ORDER inside pick_slot() and a warning in the banner --
    and the table is the only thing that decides membership.

    The asymmetry that follows, stated because it is the whole behaviour: a name
    in the socket directory that is not in the table is never offered; a name in
    the table with nothing listening still is.

    A SENTINEL RATHER THAN None FOR "READ IT YOURSELF", and the first version of
    this did use None for both. That collision means ephemeral_slots(None) --
    "here is the table, and it could not be read" -- returns the whole pool of
    whatever is on disk instead of None. assert_pool_is_typed() below went red on
    it at the first start, which is the only reason it is not in this file: the
    two meanings are indistinguishable at every call site and the wrong one
    permits.
    """
    table = slot_table() if table is _READ_IT else table
    if table is None:
        return None
    return sorted(inst for inst, kind in table if kind == "ephemeral")


def assert_pool_is_typed(read_table=slot_table, pool=ephemeral_slots):
    """Prove the mint pool comes from the typed table, at every start.

    Takes both halves as parameters for the same reason the other three do: a
    version that built its own reader could not go red for any implementation
    but the one it already trusted.

    THE ARM THAT CATCHES THE PREDECESSOR is the last one, and it is the whole
    reason this exists. Against the code this replaces -- os.listdir() on the
    proxy run directory, filtered on a ".sock" suffix -- "named0" and "zz-probe"
    are both in the pool, because the directory is where the answer came from
    and every instance on the box binds into it. Against this code they are not,
    because the answer comes from the file that carries the type. Both names are
    the measured cases from socket_filenames()'s own docstring, 2026-09-22.

    THE PERMIT ARM IS NOT OPTIONAL. A pool that returned nothing at all would
    satisfy every refusal below and would refuse every visitor on a healthy box,
    which is the exhaustion defect arriving as a fix.

    Four refusals, each a different way the old answer was wrong:

      named0     typed "desktop"  -- a person's long-lived session, in the same
                                     socket directory, offered to a stranger
      legacy     no type column   -- a table written before the column existed;
                                     defaulting it to "ephemeral" would do the
                                     same damage to every row of an old file
      zz-probe   only a filename  -- the "touch zz-probe.sock" case; it is in no
                                     table at all
      unreadable table            -- must be None and not [], because [] says
                                     "no slots are configured" and a caller
                                     cannot tell that from "I could not look"

    WHAT IT DOES NOT SEE, written down because an unlisted blind spot is read as
    exhaustive: nothing here opens a socket, reads /proc/net/unix or asks
    systemd. It is entirely about which names the pool contains. Whether one of
    them can be served is slot_unreachable()'s question and deliberately does
    NOT filter the pool -- see ephemeral_slots().
    """
    import tempfile

    d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="hdw4s-demux-pool-")
    try:
        path = os.path.join(d, "instances")
        with open(path, "w") as f:
            f.write("# an administrator's comment, and a blank line follows\n\n")
            f.write("0 eph0 ephemeral\n")
            f.write("1 named0 desktop\n")
            f.write("2 eph1 ephemeral\n")
            f.write("3 legacy\n")
            f.write("4 tmpl template\n")
        table = read_table(path)
        assert table is not None, \
            "a readable slot table was reported unreadable, so every refusal" \
            " below would pass for the wrong reason"
        got = pool(table)

        # PERMIT ARM, inside the space searched.
        assert got == ["eph0", "eph1"], \
            "the ephemeral rows of a table are %r, and the pool said %r" \
            % (["eph0", "eph1"], got)
        # REFUSE ARMS.
        assert "named0" not in got, \
            "a row typed 'desktop' was offered as ephemeral capacity: a named" \
            " desktop can be handed to a stranger"
        assert "legacy" not in got, \
            "a row with no type column was treated as ephemeral; every row of" \
            " a table written before that column existed is a named desktop"
        assert "zz-probe" not in got, \
            "a name that is in no table at all reached the pool, so the pool" \
            " is still being computed from something other than the table"
        # THE ARM THIS FEATURE RESTS ON. The administrator's authoring slot is
        # ephemeral in every mechanical respect the CLI cares about -- same
        # unit, same tmpfs home, same filesystem socket -- and the ONE thing
        # that keeps it away from strangers is that this filter names
        # "ephemeral" exactly rather than "ephemeral-shaped". Handing it out
        # gives a visitor the desktop the template is authored in, with the
        # submit control in it. There is no second check behind this one.
        assert "tmpl" not in got, \
            "a row typed 'template' was offered as ephemeral capacity: the" \
            " next visitor at the well-known hostname is handed the" \
            " administrator's authoring desktop"

        # The unreadable case, and it must be told apart from an empty one.
        missing = read_table(os.path.join(d, "no-such-file"))
        assert missing is None, \
            "an unreadable slot table was reported as a table with no rows;" \
            " a caller cannot tell 'none configured' from 'I could not look'"
        assert pool(None) is None, \
            "the pool turned an unreadable table into an empty pool, which" \
            " refuses every visitor with nothing in the log naming the file"

        # An EMPTY table is a different fact again, and must survive as one.
        empty = os.path.join(d, "empty")
        with open(empty, "w") as f:
            f.write("# nothing but a comment\n")
        assert pool(read_table(empty)) == [], \
            "an empty but readable table did not produce an empty pool"
    finally:
        import shutil
        shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)


def idle_windows(complain=None):
    """[(instance, seconds)] for every EPHEMERAL slot the pool is configured
    with; seconds is 0 for "never reaped" and None for "cannot be read".

    Zero is kept as 0 rather than folded into a large number, because the two
    need different handling and a large number would let an unbounded case
    masquerade as a bounded one. None is kept apart from both for the same
    reason one step further along.

    Read from the instance table and the conf files rather than asked of the
    service manager. That is the CLI's precedence for THIS key -- the ephemeral
    unit sets several values in Environment=, but not this one -- and it is the
    reason cookie_policy_complaints() re-reads and re-checks at runtime instead
    of trusting the start-time answer forever.

    complain is where an unreadable value is reported. It defaults to log()
    because the one thing this must never do is read a value it cannot parse
    and say nothing; pass a collector where the caller wants to place the
    message itself.
    """
    complain = log if complain is None else complain
    rows = []
    names = ephemeral_slots()
    if names is None:
        # Unchanged behaviour on purpose: this function's callers derive a
        # cookie lifetime and a refusal report, and an empty set degrades both
        # safely. The mint path asks ephemeral_slots() directly, where the
        # difference between "none configured" and "cannot read" is the
        # difference between a 503 and a 503 with the file named in the log.
        return rows
    for inst in names:
        secs, why = read_idle_window(inst)
        if why:
            complain("WARNING: %s: %s" % (inst, why))
            complain("  Nothing here will guess a window for it. Until it is"
                     " fixed this slot is treated as one that is never reaped,"
                     " which is the cautious reading and not a default.")
        rows.append((inst, secs))
    return rows


def derive_cookie_lifetime(windows):
    """Seconds an identity lasts, from the pool's own idle windows.

    A window of None -- unreadable -- contributes nothing, exactly as 0 does.
    Neither may SHORTEN the identity: an unknown window could be longer than
    any guess, so the honest answer is the floor plus a complaint, not a
    number derived from something nobody read.
    """
    longest = 0
    for _, secs in windows:
        if secs:
            longest = max(longest, secs)

    # CLAMPED TO WHAT A BROWSER WILL ACTUALLY KEEP. Without the min() this
    # returned 2 * longest unbounded, so a 500d window produced "an identity
    # lasts 1000 day(s)" -- a sentence about a cookie that has never existed.
    # Everything downstream then reasoned from it, including the check whose
    # whole job is to notice the owner being locked out.
    #
    # Clamping does not FIX a window past the crossover; nothing here can. It
    # makes the number honest, which is what lets cookie_policy_complaints()
    # see the gap -- it compares each window against this value, so handed the
    # true ceiling it reports the lockout unprompted. The repair is one
    # subtraction from the same place the defect came from.
    return min(BROWSER_COOKIE_CAP_SECONDS,
               max(COOKIE_FLOOR_SECONDS, 2 * longest))


def cookie_policy_complaints(lifetime, windows):
    """Every slot whose desktop can outlive the identity that owns it.

    This is the arm that can actually go red, and the derivation above is not.
    derive_cookie_lifetime() cannot return a value smaller than twice its own
    input, so pointing this at its own output at the same instant proves
    nothing -- a check that cannot fail by construction, which is the shape this
    file already keeps one example of on purpose.

    What makes it a real check is that the lifetime is PINNED AT START and the
    windows are not: a conf file edited afterwards, or a slot added to the pool,
    moves one clock and not the other. So this takes a lifetime and a freshly
    read set of windows and is run again later, where the two can disagree.
    """
    out = []
    for inst, secs in sorted(windows, key=lambda r: r[0]):
        if secs is None:
            out.append("%s has an idle window nobody can read, so how long its "
                       "desktop is kept is unknown and may exceed any identity;"
                       " fix the value rather than leaving it to be guessed"
                       % inst)
        elif secs <= 0:
            out.append("%s is never reaped for idleness, so its desktop can "
                       "outlive any identity; give it a nonzero idle window"
                       % inst)
        elif secs >= lifetime:
            out.append("%s keeps a desktop for %s, which is not shorter "
                       "than the %d-day identity pinned at start; restart the "
                       "front door to pick the new window up"
                       % (inst, describe_window(secs), lifetime // 86400))
    return out


# How often the check above is asked again on a running box. Not a tuning
# number: the condition it looks for is a person editing a conf file, and the
# response is a person restarting the front door, so anything inside the human
# loop is fast enough. Fifteen minutes re-reads a handful of small files on a
# tmpfs and costs nothing measurable.
COOKIE_POLICY_EVERY = 900.0


def poll_cookie_policy(seen, lifetime, windows=None, say=None):
    """Ask cookie_policy_complaints() again, and say only what CHANGED.

    THE GAP THIS CLOSES. The complaint is real, it can genuinely go red, and
    until now the only two things that ever asked it were main() at start and
    log_refusal() when the pool was exhausted. So an administrator who raises
    HDW4S_IDLE_DAYS on a running box moves the desktop clock and not the
    identity clock -- the single state this file's own essay calls
    indefensible, a desktop alive with its owner locked out -- and nothing says
    so until somebody is turned away. On a box with a spare slot that is never.
    A check whose trigger is a second failure is not a check on the first one.

    WHY IT SAYS ONLY WHAT CHANGED, and this is the whole of the design rather
    than a politeness. A timer that restated a standing complaint every fifteen
    minutes would put the same line in the log four times an hour for as long
    as the condition lasted, and a check that fires on an ordinary state gets
    turned off -- after which it protects nothing at all, which is strictly
    worse than the gap it was written for. So the state is carried in SEEN and
    only the difference is printed: silent while nothing moves, loud once at
    the moment it moves, and loud again when it clears.

    A complaint DISAPPEARING is reported too. The instrument that raised a
    question is the one that should close it; leaving the log with a warning
    and no retraction teaches a reader that the warnings do not mean anything.

    WHAT WAS REJECTED.

      An external timer unit. There is already an hdw4s-check.timer and it
      looks like the tidy home for this, and it cannot do the job: the
      comparison is between a lifetime PINNED INSIDE THIS PROCESS and the
      configuration on disk. Another process would have to guess the pinned
      value, and a check that guesses one of its two inputs is answering a
      different question. That is decisive, and it is why this is a thread.

      Firing on every arrival, rate limited. Cheaper to reason about, but it
      makes the trigger a visitor again -- a quieter visitor than a refused
      one, but the box this is written for is the box nobody is visiting.

      Watching the files for changes instead of re-reading them. It fires
      exactly when the condition can newly become true, which is elegant, and
      it buys nothing here: the answer is wanted within minutes, not
      milliseconds, and a watch adds descriptors, a failure mode when a file is
      replaced rather than written, and a second mechanism to keep true.

      Making it fatal, or restarting ourselves when the configuration moves.
      The warning's own text says the repair is to restart the front door.
      Doing that unasked would drop every live session to fix a number that
      only matters to arrivals, and the operator is the one who gets to choose
      the moment.

    Takes the windows and the reporter as parameters so it can be handed a pool
    it did not read and a collector that is not the log -- the arms below are
    exercised without a conf file, and a version that read its own inputs could
    not have been.
    """
    say = log if say is None else say
    if windows is None:
        # The default reporter is swallowed here and nowhere else. idle_windows()
        # shouts about an unreadable value, which is right on a path that runs
        # once; on a path that runs every fifteen minutes forever it is the
        # repetition this function exists to avoid. Nothing is lost: an
        # unreadable window has its own complaint in the set below, and that one
        # is printed on the transition like any other.
        windows = idle_windows(complain=lambda _m: None)
    now = set(cookie_policy_complaints(lifetime, windows))
    for complaint in sorted(now - seen):
        say("WARNING: %s" % complaint)
        say("  Nobody was turned away to produce this line. The pool's"
            " configuration has moved under an identity lifetime that was"
            " pinned at start, so a desktop can now outlive the identity that"
            " owns it -- and its owner then cannot reach it or close it.")
    gone = seen - now
    if gone:
        say("the identity-lifetime warning no longer applies (%d cleared):"
            " every configured window is now shorter than the pinned"
            " identity." % len(gone))
    return now


def watch_cookie_policy(lifetime, seen=None, every=COOKIE_POLICY_EVERY):
    """Run poll_cookie_policy() forever. Started as a daemon thread by main().

    SEEN is seeded with what main() already printed, so a standing complaint is
    stated once at start rather than again fifteen minutes later.

    It sleeps FIRST and swallows nothing but its own failure to read: this runs
    beside the accept loop, and a thread that dies takes the check with it
    silently, which is the shape of defect the whole file is about. A failure
    to read the pool is therefore reported and the loop continues.
    """
    seen = set() if seen is None else set(seen)
    while True:
        time.sleep(every)
        try:
            seen = poll_cookie_policy(seen, lifetime)
        except Exception as e:      # noqa: BLE001 -- see above
            log("WARNING: the identity-lifetime re-check failed: %r" % e)
            log("  It will be tried again, but until it succeeds nothing is"
                " watching for a widened idle window.")


def assert_cookie_outlives_idle(derive=derive_cookie_lifetime):
    """Prove the derivation, in BOTH directions, at every start.

    Takes the derivation as a PARAMETER for the same reason
    assert_ownership_keying() takes a factory: a version that built its own
    answer and checked that could not fail for any implementation but the one it
    already trusted. Handed the value that ships today -- a flat seven days,
    equal to the default idle window -- this goes red, and that is how it was
    seen to go red rather than merely written.
    """
    # THE SHARP ONE FIRST, deliberately. The floor checks below are also real,
    # but a broken derivation usually trips both, and whichever runs first is
    # the message the reader gets. "Under the floor" is a tuning complaint;
    # "the owner is locked out of a running desktop" is the defect, and that is
    # what a red arm here should say.
    #
    # For every window a pool could plausibly carry, the identity must outlive
    # the desktop -- strictly, not equally. Equal is the shipped defect: the
    # person is locked out at the moment the desktop is still there.
    # In SECONDS, and the sub-day probes are not decoration. The windows were
    # whole days here, so 12h and 90m -- forms the tool accepts and the manual
    # names -- could not be represented at all: they truncated to zero, which
    # this file reads as "never reaped". A repair that only fixed the parsing
    # would have turned a silent week into a silent forever.
    # THE CEILING OF THIS LIST WAS THE SECOND HALF OF THE DEFECT. It stopped at
    # 31536000 -- 365 days -- which sits BELOW the 400-day browser cap, so every
    # probe landed in the region where the derivation happens to be unclamped
    # and the check could not reach the case it exists to find. A guard whose
    # inputs all avoid the failing region passes forever without being wrong
    # about any of them.
    for secs in (5400, 43200, 86400, 604800, 1209600, 2592000, 7776000,
                 31536000, 34560000, 43200000):
        got = derive([("probe", secs)])
        # THE CROSSOVER IS THE CAP ITSELF, not half of it. Written first as
        # `secs * 2 <= cap`, which is where the clamp starts BITING -- but a
        # clamped 400-day identity still outlives a 365-day desktop perfectly
        # well. That version demanded a complaint about a pool that is fine,
        # and the suite said so. The guarantee survives right up to the cap.
        if secs < BROWSER_COOKIE_CAP_SECONDS:
            assert got > secs, (
                "an identity lasting %d second(s) does not outlive a desktop "
                "kept for %d second(s): the owner is locked out of a session "
                "that is still running" % (got, secs))
            continue

        # PAST THE CROSSOVER THE GUARANTEE IS NOT AVAILABLE, and saying so is
        # the honest arm rather than a weaker one. No value we return can make
        # an identity outlive a desktop kept longer than the browser will hold
        # the cookie. Two things must then be true, and BOTH are checked
        # because either alone is satisfiable by a broken derivation:
        #
        #   the number must be clamped -- claiming more is describing a cookie
        #   that does not exist, which is precisely what shipped;
        #   and the operator must be TOLD -- a clamp on its own converts a loud
        #   wrong number into a quiet right one, which is worse.
        assert got <= BROWSER_COOKIE_CAP_SECONDS, (
            "the derivation claimed an identity of %d second(s), longer than "
            "the %d second(s) a browser will keep the cookie: a promise about "
            "a cookie that does not exist"
            % (got, BROWSER_COOKIE_CAP_SECONDS))
        assert cookie_policy_complaints(got, [("probe", secs)]), (
            "a desktop kept for %d second(s) outlives the %d second(s) the "
            "browser keeps the identity, and nothing complained"
            % (secs, got))

    # PERMIT ARM. A pool with nothing to derive from, one whose slots are never
    # reaped at all, and one whose window cannot be read, must each still
    # produce a lifetime rather than zero.
    assert derive([]) >= COOKIE_FLOOR_SECONDS, \
        "an empty pool produced no identity lifetime at all"
    assert derive([("a", 0)]) >= COOKIE_FLOOR_SECONDS, \
        "a pool that is never reaped produced no identity lifetime at all"
    assert derive([("a", None)]) >= COOKIE_FLOOR_SECONDS, \
        "a pool with an unreadable window produced no identity lifetime at all"

    # And the floor is a floor, not a value: a pool with a long window must
    # raise it rather than be clamped to it.
    assert derive([("probe", 90 * 86400)]) > COOKIE_FLOOR_SECONDS, \
        "a long idle window was clamped to the floor"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ownership
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Ownership:
    """identity -> the sessions it owns, and sid -> the slot behind it.

    Append-only by construction, because the ruling is that sharing is additive
    and nothing is ever revoked. The only pruning is of sids whose session is
    gone, and pruning one NEVER hands its slot to the sid's old owner.
    """

    def __init__(self):
        self._lock = threading.Lock()
        # sid -> {"identity", "instance", "minted"}
        self._by_sid = {}
        # identity -> [sid, ...] in mint order
        self._by_identity = {}
        # identity -> when we last wound its cookie on. Purely a rate limit on
        # Set-Cookie headers, never an authority on anything: losing it on a
        # restart costs one extra header per identity and nothing else, which is
        # why it is allowed to live here rather than on disk.
        self._refreshed = {}

    def due_for_refresh(self, identity, now=None, every=COOKIE_REFRESH_EVERY):
        """True when the next response to this identity should carry a cookie.

        Says yes the first time it is asked about an identity, so a browser
        whose cookie predates a restart is wound on immediately rather than
        after an hour of holding a cookie nobody here has refreshed.
        """
        now = time.time() if now is None else now
        with self._lock:
            last = self._refreshed.get(identity)
            if last is not None and now - last < every:
                return False
            # Bounded, because an open front door can be handed an unlimited
            # number of distinct cookies by anything that can reach it, and this
            # dict is the only structure here that a stranger can grow without
            # owning a session. Dropping the table costs one redundant header.
            if len(self._refreshed) > 20000:
                self._refreshed.clear()
            self._refreshed[identity] = now
            return True

    def mint_identity(self):
        return secrets.token_hex(16)

    def mint_session(self, identity, instance):
        """Record that IDENTITY now holds INSTANCE. No desktop exists yet."""
        sid = secrets.token_hex(16)
        with self._lock:
            self._by_sid[sid] = {
                "identity": identity,
                "instance": instance,
                "minted": time.time(),
                # Filled in by attach() once a desktop has actually answered.
                "live": False,
                "incarnation": None,
                # WHY A SESSION ENDED, SET BY US, AND WHY IT IS NOT DERIVED.
                # Every other signal that a letting is over is read from the
                # world -- a runtime directory, a listening port, a published
                # token -- and every one of them goes on saying "healthy" for
                # seconds after a teardown has been asked for. Measured on
                # production 2026-09-26: a discard at 14:33:45, the session
                # gone at 14:33:48, and in between the visitor's own tab
                # retried, was proxied, and SOCKET-ACTIVATED A FRESH DESKTOP in
                # the slot it had just released. This field is the one fact no
                # instrument can be late about, because it is the act itself.
                "ended": None,
            "ended_at": None,
            }
            self._by_identity.setdefault(identity, []).append(sid)
        return sid

    def attach(self, sid, incarnation):
        """Record that a desktop has answered for SID, and which start it is.

        NOT DONE AT MINT, and the first version did it there and gated every
        visitor in the pool. A mint precedes its session: the redirect is what
        sends the browser at the slot, and the session is socket-activated by
        that first request. So at mint time the token on disk belongs to the
        PREVIOUS occupant -- it survives a reap, measured -- and the session
        that is about to start will publish a different one. Comparing against
        the value read at mint therefore refused every brand-new session as
        "replaced", which is the check working perfectly on the wrong instant.

        Written once and then left alone. Refreshing it would make it agree
        with whatever is there now, which is the question and not the answer.
        """
        with self._lock:
            rec = self._by_sid.get(sid)
            if rec is None or rec["live"]:
                return
            rec["live"] = True
            rec["incarnation"] = incarnation

    def end_session(self, sid, why):
        """Record that this letting is OVER, from here on, whatever the box says.

        THE ORDERING DEFECT THIS EXISTS FOR, and it is why the record is marked
        rather than dropped. A visitor who discards their desktop has a tab
        still open on it, and that tab retries about every five seconds by
        design. The teardown is asynchronous -- the router may not perform it,
        see request_teardown() -- so for a second or three the slot's runtime
        directory is still there, its port is still listening and its published
        token still matches. Every guard in session_replaced() therefore reads
        HEALTHY, the request is proxied, and the proxy's connect() is an
        INSTRUCTION to systemd rather than a question: BindsTo= starts a whole
        new GNOME session for nobody. On production, where nothing sets
        HDW4S_IDLE_DAYS and the shipped default is seven days, that desktop
        then holds a slot for a week with the reaper correctly saying nothing.

        MARKED, NOT FORGOTTEN, so the visitor's own session directory can still
        say "discarding" about it. The record is dropped at the gate, on the
        first request that presents it, exactly as a reaped one is.
        """
        with self._lock:
            rec = self._by_sid.get(sid)
            if rec is not None and not rec["ended"]:
                rec["ended"] = why
                # WHEN it ended, because the two readers of this flag need
                # different lifetimes and sharing one was a defect. The
                # resurrection guard must never forget -- a stale tab may return
                # at any time, and forgetting is what let it mint. The visitor's
                # directory must forget, because a row that says "discarding"
                # for ever is a row about work that finished in seconds.
                # Measured on production 2026-09-26: a slot discarded at 19:51
                # was still listed as discarding twenty-one minutes later, with
                # its teardown long complete and the marker directory empty.
                rec["ended_at"] = time.time()
            return rec is not None

    def pending_mints(self, since):
        """[(instance, minted)] for slots handed out whose desktop has not
        answered yet, and only while that is still plausible.

        The half of occupancy that is ours, and it is deliberately BOTH narrow
        and short. A mint precedes the session it starts, so for a few seconds a
        slot is legitimately held on our word alone. Two bounds, and leaving out
        either one reintroduces a defect:

          not live   Once a desktop has answered, the authority says so and our
                     word is not merely unnecessary, it is WRONG. Without this
                     a slot the sweep reaped seconds after it was let stayed
                     held for the rest of the grace window, so a pool that had
                     just been freed still answered 503 -- measured against the
                     arm for exactly that, which went red with the whole window
                     applied rather than only its pending part.
          recent     Without this the table grows forever, which is how
                     instances_in_use() became monotonic and why every visitor
                     past the Nth got 503 for the life of the process.
        """
        with self._lock:
            return [(r["instance"], r["minted"]) for r in self._by_sid.values()
                    if not r["live"] and r["minted"] >= since]

    def lookup(self, sid):
        with self._lock:
            rec = self._by_sid.get(sid)
            return dict(rec) if rec else None

    def owned_by(self, identity):
        with self._lock:
            return list(self._by_identity.get(identity, ()))

    def instances_in_use(self):
        """The slots this router has a LIVE letting for. Never the ones it had.

        ENDED LETTINGS ARE NOT LETTINGS, and leaving them in was visible in
        production: the line "3 slot(s) now let" counted a slot whose desktop
        had been discarded seconds earlier and was already stopping. Nothing
        admits a visitor on this number -- slots_in_use() does that, from the
        authority -- but an operator reads it, and a count that only ever goes
        up is the monotonic defect this table has had once already, wearing a
        log line instead of a 503.
        """
        with self._lock:
            return {r["instance"] for r in self._by_sid.values()
                    if not r.get("ended")}

    def holders_of(self, instance, besides=None):
        """Every sid whose letting names INSTANCE, other than BESIDES.

        ASKED RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM CALL ORDER, and that is the whole reason
        it is a method here instead of a set difference at the call site. The
        one caller wants "has anybody ELSE been let this slot", and it runs
        beside a forget() of its own sid -- so a version written as "is this
        instance in instances_in_use()" would answer correctly only while the
        forget stayed on the right side of it, and would start answering "yes,
        me" the day somebody moved one line. The answer it would then give
        permits nothing, but the call site that depends on it DESTROYS a
        desktop, and a guard whose correctness is a property of statement order
        is not a guard.
        """
        with self._lock:
            return [s for s, r in self._by_sid.items()
                    if r["instance"] == instance and s != besides
                    # AN ENDED LETTING DOES NOT HOLD A SLOT, and counting one
                    # disarmed the reclaim completely. Measured on production
                    # 2026-09-26: "did not reclaim a slot: ephemeral0 has been
                    # let to somebody else since", about a slot let twice to the
                    # SAME visitor, where the somebody else was their own
                    # discarded session still sitting in this table. Nothing
                    # ever removed it, so every slot that had been let twice was
                    # permanently unreclaimable -- the leak's second half, and
                    # it hid behind a refusal that reads like the guard working.
                    and not r.get("ended")]

    def forget(self, sid):
        """Drop a sid whose session is gone. Its slot is NOT reassigned here."""
        with self._lock:
            rec = self._by_sid.pop(sid, None)
            if rec:
                owned = self._by_identity.get(rec["identity"])
                if owned and sid in owned:
                    owned.remove(sid)
            return rec


def assert_ownership_keying(factory=Ownership):
    """Prove the recycling guard, in BOTH directions, at every start.

    A guard that has never rejected anything is not known to reject anything --
    and a guard that refuses everything would also look green, so this asserts
    the permit arm as well as the refuse arm. It runs at startup rather than
    only in a test file, because the failure it prevents is silent and
    unrecoverable: a stranger's desktop handed over by our own ownership check.

    It takes a FACTORY rather than nothing, and that is the whole point. The
    first version of this function built its own Ownership() and ignored what it
    was handed, so it could not fail for any implementation but the one it
    already trusted -- a check that cannot fail by construction, which is the
    exact defect it exists to catch. It was caught by pointing it at a
    deliberately slot-keyed table and finding it still green. Keep the
    parameter: without it this is decoration.
    """
    probe = factory()

    a = probe.mint_identity()
    b = probe.mint_identity()
    assert a != b, "two identities minted the same value"

    # A owns a session on a slot.
    sid_a = probe.mint_session(a, "ephemeral0")

    # PERMIT ARM. A's own sid must still resolve to A's session. Without this a
    # guard that simply refused everything would pass the arm below.
    rec = probe.lookup(sid_a)
    assert rec and rec["identity"] == a and rec["instance"] == "ephemeral0", \
        "the owner of a live session was not routed to it"

    # The session is reaped and the SLOT is recycled to B.
    probe.forget(sid_a)
    sid_b = probe.mint_session(b, "ephemeral0")

    # REFUSE ARM. A returns with its cookie and its old address.
    assert probe.lookup(sid_a) is None, \
        "a reaped session's address still resolves"
    assert sid_a not in probe.owned_by(a), \
        "a reaped session is still listed as owned"

    # And the sharp one: A must not reach B's desktop, although B is now on the
    # very slot A used to hold. This is the assertion that goes red the moment
    # anyone keys the table on the slot instead of the session instance.
    assert sid_b != sid_a, "a recycled slot reissued the same session address"
    rec_b = probe.lookup(sid_b)
    assert rec_b["identity"] == b, "the recycled slot's session is not B's"
    for sid in probe.owned_by(a):
        assert probe.lookup(sid)["identity"] == a, \
            "identity A owns a session belonging to someone else"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything this process remembers, and what each piece of it is FOR
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The two guards above each prove one invariant. This one proves something
# weaker and broader, and it exists because of what the two of them cannot see:
# they say nothing at all about state that did not exist when they were written.
#
# THE FAILURE IT IS AIMED AT. Every defect this file carries a paragraph about
# is the same shape -- two records of one fact drifting apart, or one record
# being read as a different kind of thing than it is. The cookie was a fuse
# because a lifetime was read as a sliding window. The ownership table was
# nearly keyed on the slot because an address was read as an identity. A
# desktop's last-request time and the sweep's sample of it are two clocks that
# disagree, and saying so out loud is the whole of log_refusal(). In each case
# the code was correct line by line and the mistake was in what the state MEANT.
#
# So the inventory below records the meaning, in three kinds, and the kinds are
# not decoration -- they are what tells a reader whether losing the thing is a
# cost or a catastrophe:
#
#   authority     Somebody is refused or admitted on the strength of it. Losing
#                 it loses a person their desktop. It may never be rebuilt from
#                 a guess, and a second copy of it is a second answer.
#   cache         An answer we could recompute. Losing it costs time and
#                 nothing else. If losing one can change a decision it was
#                 never a cache, and calling it one is how the mistake starts.
#   rate limiter  A record of when we last did something, kept only to avoid
#                 doing it too often. Losing it costs one redundant action.
#                 The tell is that clearing it wholesale must be harmless --
#                 due_for_refresh() does exactly that when it grows too large,
#                 which is only sound BECAUSE it is this kind.
#
#   not state     Listed although it records nothing, because the alternative
#                 to listing it is an exemption no reader can see, and an
#                 unwritten exemption is the same defect as an unlisted
#                 container. Two of them: the one write this module makes into
#                 somebody else's namespace, and this table itself, which is a
#                 dict and was duly reported by its own first run.
#
# WHY A LIST AND NOT A CONVENTION. Whoever adds the next container will also be
# correct line by line. The list is cheap to satisfy and impossible to satisfy
# by accident: it cannot be filled in without answering "which of the three is
# this", and that question is the one nobody asked about the cookie or the
# ownership key.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLAIM, because a list read as exhaustive is
# worse than no list -- it silently permits whatever it forgot:
#
#   * Only module-level and class-level state is inventoried, plus the
#     attributes one instance of the ownership table is born with. State a
#     function builds and hands to something that keeps it is invisible here.
#   * An imported module's namespace is not walked. DURATION is the grammar
#     this file reads its idle windows with; nothing here writes to it, and
#     nothing here would notice if something did.
#   * A lock is not state and is skipped by type. A lock guards an answer, it
#     never holds one, and losing one costs no answer -- but note what that
#     means: this cannot see a lock protecting the wrong table.
#   * It is a bookkeeping check. It asks whether somebody wrote down what a
#     piece of state is for. It cannot tell whether what they wrote is TRUE.

STATE_KINDS = ("authority", "cache", "rate limiter", "not state")

STATE_INVENTORY = {
    # Who owns which session. The refusal in serve() is decided from this, and
    # it is the reason a reaped slot does not hand a stranger a desktop.
    "Ownership._by_sid": "authority",
    # The other direction of the same fact, and the one arrival() resumes from.
    "Ownership._by_identity": "authority",
    # When each identity's cookie was last wound on. Cleared wholesale above
    # the bound; the cost of that is one redundant Set-Cookie header.
    "Ownership._refreshed": "rate limiter",
    # When each slot's last-request file was last rewritten. Losing it costs
    # one extra write to a tmpfs.
    "_touched": "rate limiter",
    # Rebound once, by main(), from the pool the box is configured with. An
    # authority: it is the Max-Age a browser is handed, so a wrong value locks
    # somebody out of a running desktop. Pinned on purpose -- and pinned is
    # exactly why cookie_policy_complaints() has to be asked again against a
    # freshly read pool rather than against the value it was derived from.
    "COOKIE_LIFETIME": "authority",
    # Set by load_duration() so that importing the grammar beside this script
    # cannot leave a .pyc behind. An interpreter flag, not a record of
    # anything, and listed rather than exempted.
    "sys.dont_write_bytecode": "not state",
    # This table. It is a dict, so the sweep found it on its first run and said
    # so -- which is the sweep working, not a nuisance: any command that
    # searches for its own subject can match itself, and the cheap escape
    # would have been a special case that also hid the next table somebody
    # adds. Written once at import and never assigned to afterwards.
    "STATE_INVENTORY": "not state",
    # A default-argument sentinel for ephemeral_slots(). An identity to compare
    # against and nothing else: nothing is ever written into it and nothing
    # reads anything out of it. It is a bare object() rather than a string or
    # None precisely so that it cannot collide with a real answer -- which is
    # the defect it was introduced to fix.
    "_READ_IT": "not state",
}


def _frozen(value):
    """True when nothing can be written into VALUE.

    A tuple is only as immutable as what is in it, so this recurses rather than
    trusting the outer type: a tuple of dicts is a mutable container wearing an
    immutable label, and that is precisely the disguise this check exists to
    see through.
    """
    if isinstance(value, (bool, int, float, complex, str, bytes, bytearray,
                          type(None), range, slice)):
        return not isinstance(value, bytearray)
    if isinstance(value, (tuple, frozenset)):
        return all(_frozen(v) for v in value)
    return False


def _not_state(value):
    """True for things that are code or a lock rather than a record.

    Functions, classes and modules are what the file IS; a lock is how a record
    is guarded rather than a record. Classes are walked separately for the
    attributes hung on them, so skipping them here is not skipping them.
    """
    import types
    if isinstance(value, (types.FunctionType, types.BuiltinFunctionType,
                          types.MethodType, types.ModuleType, type)):
        return True
    return isinstance(value, (type(threading.Lock()), type(threading.RLock()),
                              threading.Condition, threading.Event,
                              threading.Semaphore))


def uninventoried_state(ns, cls=None, inventory=None, source=None,
                        examined=None):
    """Every piece of this module's state that nobody has said the purpose of.

    REPORTS rather than refuses, and that split is deliberate. The test tier
    asserts on what comes back and goes red; the router logs it and starts.
    Those are opposite needs and the wrong way to serve them is a mode flag,
    because the flag is then a check that can be wired wrong in the quiet
    direction -- set to "warn" in the suite, which would go green while this
    printed violations into a log nobody reads. There is no flag. There is one
    function that returns a list, and each caller is visibly one or the other.

    It takes the NAMESPACE rather than finding its own, for the same reason
    assert_ownership_keying() takes a factory and assert_cookie_outlives_idle()
    takes a derivation: a version that inspected only itself could not fail for
    any module but the one it already trusted. Handed a namespace with an extra
    dict in it, this goes red, and that is how it was seen to go red.

    A dict is also the right handle for the caller to be given. Building a
    module with an unlisted container in it, to prove the check can see one,
    should not require editing the module under test -- we have already had a
    red arm on this project that mutated the checker along with the code and
    proved nothing.

    EXAMINED, if a list is given, collects every name this actually looked
    at. It exists so the caller can say out loud that the sweep RAN rather
    than that it found nothing -- and so that sentence cannot be written
    without calling this. A count of the inventory's own length would have
    been restatable by a main() that had deleted the call, and was:
    measured, by deleting it and watching the suite stay green.
    """
    import ast
    cls = ns.get("Ownership") if cls is None else cls
    inventory = STATE_INVENTORY if inventory is None else inventory
    out = []
    found = set()
    examined = [] if examined is None else examined

    def check(name, value):
        found.add(name)
        examined.append(name)
        if _frozen(value) or _not_state(value):
            return
        if name not in inventory:
            out.append("%s is a %s that nothing has classified; add it to "
                       "STATE_INVENTORY as one of %s, or it is state nobody "
                       "has decided the meaning of"
                       % (name, type(value).__name__, ", ".join(STATE_KINDS)))

    # 1. Module globals, as they stand after import.
    mod_name = ns.get("__name__")
    for k, v in sorted(ns.items()):
        if k.startswith("__"):
            continue
        check(k, v)

    # 2. Attributes hung on the classes this module defines. A dict in a class
    #    body is shared by every instance ever made, which is the sharpest
    #    version of the defect and the one a globals walk cannot see: it does
    #    not look like a global and it does not look like instance state.
    for k, v in sorted(ns.items()):
        if isinstance(v, type) and getattr(v, "__module__", None) == mod_name:
            for a, av in sorted(vars(v).items()):
                if a.startswith("__") or callable(av):
                    continue
                check("%s.%s" % (k, a), av)

    # 3. What one ownership table is born with. Built rather than read off the
    #    class, because these attributes exist only once __init__ has run.
    if cls is not None:
        probe = cls()
        for a, av in sorted(vars(probe).items()):
            # Matched against every name in the inheritance chain, so a
            # subclass inherits its base's classifications and only an
            # attribute NOBODY has classified is reported. Without this a
            # harness that subclasses Ownership to add one dict would be told
            # about all four.
            label = "%s.%s" % (type(probe).__name__, a)
            for base in type(probe).__mro__:
                if "%s.%s" % (base.__name__, a) in inventory:
                    label = "%s.%s" % (base.__name__, a)
                    break
            check(label, av)

    # 4 and 5 read the SOURCE, because two kinds of mutable state leave no
    #   trace in a namespace walk: a global that is rebound at run time (its
    #   value may be an ordinary number) and a write into another module's
    #   namespace. The file parsed is the one this namespace was loaded from,
    #   derived and never named -- a constant path here would be the "which
    #   copy is running" trap inside the check written to notice it.
    path = ns.get("__file__") if source is None else source
    tree = None
    if path:
        try:
            with open(path, "r") as f:
                tree = ast.parse(f.read(), filename=path)
        except (OSError, SyntaxError) as e:
            # A detector that could not run says so. Silence here would read
            # exactly like a clean sweep, which is the one thing it must not.
            out.append("the source at %s could not be read or parsed (%s), so "
                       "rebound globals and writes into other modules were not "
                       "checked at all -- this is not a clean result" % (path, e))
    else:
        out.append("this namespace does not say which file it came from, so "
                   "rebound globals and writes into other modules were not "
                   "checked at all -- this is not a clean result")

    if tree is not None:
        imported = set()
        for n in ast.walk(tree):
            if isinstance(n, ast.Import):
                for a in n.names:
                    imported.add((a.asname or a.name).split(".")[0])
            elif isinstance(n, ast.ImportFrom):
                for a in n.names:
                    imported.add(a.asname or a.name)
        for n in ast.walk(tree):
            # 4. A global that some function rebinds. COOKIE_LIFETIME is a
            #    number, so no walk of values could ever have found it.
            if isinstance(n, ast.Global):
                for name in n.names:
                    found.add(name)
                    examined.append(name)
                    if name not in inventory:
                        out.append("%s is a module global rebound at run time "
                                   "(line %d) and nothing has classified it; "
                                   "a value that changes after start is state "
                                   "however simple it looks"
                                   % (name, n.lineno))
            # 5. A write into an imported module's namespace. That state
            #    outlives this module and is shared with everything else in
            #    the interpreter, so it is the least visible of the lot.
            targets = []
            if isinstance(n, ast.Assign):
                targets = n.targets
            elif isinstance(n, (ast.AugAssign, ast.AnnAssign)):
                targets = [n.target]
            for t in targets:
                if isinstance(t, ast.Attribute) and \
                        isinstance(t.value, ast.Name) and \
                        t.value.id in imported:
                    name = "%s.%s" % (t.value.id, t.attr)
                    found.add(name)
                    examined.append(name)
                    if name not in inventory:
                        out.append("%s is written at line %d, which puts state "
                                   "in another module's namespace; nothing has "
                                   "classified it" % (name, n.lineno))

    # 6. THE OTHER DIRECTION, and it is the one that rots quietly. An entry
    #    naming something that has been removed or renamed still reads as a
    #    considered decision about a live piece of state. Nothing else will
    #    ever surface it: the thing it describes is simply not there to
    #    contradict it, so every check keeps passing.
    for name in sorted(inventory):
        if name not in found:
            out.append("STATE_INVENTORY classifies %s as %r, and no such state "
                       "exists any more; the classification is describing "
                       "something that has been removed or renamed"
                       % (name, inventory[name]))

    # 7. And a classification has to be one of the kinds, or the list records
    #    a word rather than a decision.
    for name, kind in sorted(inventory.items()):
        if kind not in STATE_KINDS:
            out.append("STATE_INVENTORY classifies %s as %r, which is not one "
                       "of %s" % (name, kind, ", ".join(STATE_KINDS)))
    return out


def assert_state_is_inventoried(ns, cls=None, inventory=None):
    """The refusing face of uninventoried_state(), for the test tier.

    The router does NOT call this; it logs what the reporter returns and
    starts. An unclassified container is a mistake made while editing this
    file, not a condition a visitor or an administrator can create, so paying
    for it with a front door that will not come up is the wrong direction --
    and a guard that takes the service down over bookkeeping is removed inside
    a week, after which it guards nothing.

    That is only safe because the suite runs THIS, and asserts on the refusal
    itself rather than on an exit status. If it did not, the two faces would be
    a mode selector with nobody watching the quiet setting.
    """
    complaints = uninventoried_state(ns, cls, inventory)
    assert not complaints, "state nobody has classified:\n  " + \
        "\n  ".join(complaints)


